  "state": {
    "case_id": "44",
    "case_summary": "In August 2003, a 20-year-old woman, 16 weeks pregnant and mother of one, visited the orthopaedic outpatient clinic due to a 2-month history of a slowly enlarging, painless mass in the right hypothenar eminence. She is right-hand dominant and a housewife. There was no history of trauma, insect bites, or drainage from the area. Her past medical and surgical history was unremarkable, and she was not on any prescribed medication. There was no family history of tumors, and she denied smoking, alcohol, and drug abuse. Physical examination revealed a non-tender, firm, well-demarcated mass tethered to the subcutaneous tissues, surrounded by mild erythema, measuring approximately 2.5 cm × 3.0 cm. No palpable lymph nodes were found at the ipsilateral elbow or axilla, and no other abnormal signs were noted. Haematological and biochemical investigations were within normal limits. A plain X-ray of the right hand and forearm showed no abnormalities. Magnetic resonance imaging indicated that the mass infiltrated all skin layers and encircled the ulnar artery without affecting the underlying bones. The absence of a signal void within the mass ruled out certain vascular conditions. Ultrasound-guided needle biopsy revealed areas consistent with collagenous fibrous tissue of varying cellularity, with minimal mitotic activity. Immunohistochemistry showed focal positivity for smooth muscle actin (SMA), indicating myofibroblastic differentiation. Histological findings were consistent with fibromatosis.\n\nHistology showed a multi-nodular, well-circumscribed uniform spindle cell lesion with varying cellularity and fibrosis. In one section, central cyst formation was observed. Occasional mitoses of normal morphology were seen. Necrosis was not a feature. In several areas, the lesion appeared to be intravascular, the vessel was lined by an apparent endothelium. Scattered osteoclast-like multi-nucleate giant cells were also observed. Immunohistochemistry replicated diffuse positivity for SMA, but not for desmin, CD34 or S100 protein. Appearances were consistent with a myofibroblastic proliferation.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
